      "cat": "GEOPOLITICS",
      "headline": "TRUMP DECLARES IRAN CEASEFIRE 'OVER' AS U.S. HITS ~140 TARGETS, REIGNITING THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ RISK PREMIUM",
      "body": "President Trump said the ceasefire ending the Iran war is 'over' after Tehran was accused of attacking three commercial ships in the Strait of Hormuz; the U.S. military responded with strikes on roughly 140 Iranian targets early Sunday — missile and drone sites, naval assets, ammunition depots, and coastal surveillance — on top of about 90 targets hit earlier in the week. Iran's navy says it fired on a container vessel that ignored an 'unapproved route' warning, leaving one crew member missing and the ship damaged by fire; markets are closed until Monday, so none of this is priced in yet.",
      "explain": "The Strait of Hormuz carries roughly 20% of global oil and gas trade through a chokepoint only about two miles wide at its narrowest shipping lane, so any credible threat to transit — not just an actual blockade — forces tanker owners and insurers to reprice the probability of disruption immediately. Friday's WTI close of $71.90 already reflected a market that had priced most of the war premium out on diplomatic optimism; strikes and a declared end to the ceasefire over the weekend reset that probability higher just as futures reopen Monday, which is why Sunday's escalation matters more for markets than its dollar value in damage."
