      "cat": "COMMODITIES",
      "headline": "OIL'S WAR-RISK PREMIUM WHIPSAWS AGAIN: WTI TRADED NEAR $75 MIDWEEK, EASED TO $71.90 FRIDAY, NOW SET UP FOR A GAP HIGHER MONDAY",
      "body": "WTI eased about 1.0% to $71.90 and Brent slipped toward $76 on Friday as Trump's Iran-outreach comments deflated the risk premium built up when crude spiked toward $75 (WTI) and $79 (Brent) earlier in the week on tanker attacks near Hormuz. Analysts had already warned the move looked overdone — strategist Fabien Yip noted Brent had 'nearly unwound its entire war premium, despite an MoU with no enforcement details and ongoing strikes' — and Sunday's ceasefire collapse looks likely to validate that call when futures reopen.",
      "explain": "Oil's war-risk premium is priced off the probability of a specific tail scenario — a closed strait, seized tankers, mined shipping lanes — multiplied by its impact, so it swings on headlines far faster than physical supply-demand fundamentals do; that's the mechanical reason WTI can move 1% on a diplomatic quote and then need to reprice again days later when the underlying facts change. Shipping-side data backs this up: tanker crossings had only partially recovered even during the 'calmer' stretch, since war-risk insurance and owner caution lag the price action, meaning the physical market was already more cautious than Friday's $71.90 print suggested."
    },
    {
      "cat": "MACRO",
      "headline": "TUESDAY'S CPI WALKS INTO A FRESH OIL SHOCK, WITH THE JULY 28-29 FOMC MEETING STILL THE BIGGER PRIZE",
      "body": "The 10-year Treasury yield eased 3 bps to 4.55% Friday and the 2-year held at 4.19%, pricing a 10s2s spread of about 36 bps on the assumption that oil's retreat would keep a lid on near-term inflation risk heading into Tuesday's June CPI report. A weekend oil shock complicates that setup: if WTI gaps higher Monday on the Iran escalation, it raises the odds that energy adds back into the headline print even though futures pricing still shows close to zero chance of a 2026 rate cut.",
      "explain": "Headline CPI captures retail gasoline prices within two to three weeks, so a weekend jump in crude shows up fast in the number the market watches, while core CPI — the Fed's preferred gauge — strips energy out entirely and moves far more slowly on shelter and services costs. That split matters because a hot headline print driven purely by an oil shock is unlikely to move the Fed's July 28-29 decision on its own, but it can still move the long end of the curve if traders can't immediately tell whether an inflation surprise is energy-driven or broad-based."
    },
